#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# ready-and-idle — find open change requests that are DONE and UNATTENDED.
#
# A "ready-and-idle" change request is one that is open, not a draft, green on
# its CURRENT head, has no unresolved blocking discussion and no unmet required
# approval — and has had no activity for --idle-hours. Nothing is waiting on a
# human and nothing is waiting on CI: it is finished work that nobody is
# carrying. Every second in this state is pure lead time, and it accrues rebase
# debt against the target branch for free.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (measured, 2026-09-21)
#
# Across three slow walt_ui tickets, 91.8% of the combined `code` phase —
# 610,039s, 7.06 days — was exactly this state: !1187 idle 210,960s (93.6%),
# !1189 idle 202,771s (94.1%), !1190 idle 196,308s (87.9%). A fleet run opened
# them, merged its other MRs, and ended while still holding these three.
# Nothing noticed for 2.5 days. Draining them later cost 7 extra pipeline runs
# and 3 extra re-reviews, because main had moved under them (!1187 alone: 19
# commits, 4 full pipelines).
#
# The structural hole this fills: `ai/bin/lead-time` only ever queries MERGED
# change requests, so an orphaned OPEN one is invisible to it until it merges.
# lead-time is a postmortem instrument and it reported this 2.5 days after the
# damage was unrecoverable. This is the same measurement taken while the work
# is still recoverable, so `drift` is a bill you can still refuse to pay.
#
# READ-ONLY. It merges nothing, approves nothing, comments nothing, resolves
# nothing, relabels nothing. Reads go through plain `gh`/`glab` (the Athena
# wrapper is for WRITES, and there are none here). The one thing it may write
# is `git fetch`, into the local repo's remote-tracking refs, to make `drift`
# measurable; `--no-fetch` skips it.
#
# CROSS-FORGE: the repo's `origin` remote selects the backend, the same way
# lead-time picks one —
#   github.com -> gh   (gen_saas, custom)
#   gitlab.com -> glab (walt_ui)
#
# Deliberately gem-free (stdlib only); run with bare `ruby`.
#
# Usage:
#   ready-and-idle --repo DIR [--idle-hours N] [--json] [--no-fetch]
#   ready-and-idle --self-test                # pure logic, no network, no git
#
# Exit: 0 ok (INCLUDING a clean scan that found nothing) — rows are complete
#         and every field was measured,
#       1 usage/tool error — nothing ran,
#       3 SCAN INCOMPLETE — NO rows emitted; MEMBERSHIP could not be
#         established, so this run measured nothing and MUST NOT be read as
#         "zero orphans". Report it as UNAVAILABLE,
#       4 DEGRADED SCAN — rows ARE emitted and membership is COMPLETE and
#         trustworthy (0 rows here still means 0 orphans); one or more rows'
#         `drift` is a lower bound (`>=N`) or uncountable (`n/a`). Act on the
#         rows; never quote a `>=N` drift as exact.
#
# For a caller that reads only the exit code: 3 means "I have no list", 4 means
# "I have the list, one column is soft".

require "time"
require "json"
require "open3"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pure core (self-tested; no network, no git)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

module ReadyAndIdle
  module_function

  # Seconds since `iso`, or nil if the timestamp is missing/unparseable. An
  # unparseable timestamp is NOT "0 seconds ago" and is NOT "infinitely idle" —
  # it is an absent measurement, and `disqualifier` treats it as such.
  def idle_seconds(iso, now)
    return nil if iso.nil? || iso.to_s.empty?

    (now - Time.iso8601(iso.to_s)).to_i
  rescue ArgumentError
    nil
  end

  # Whole-number, human-scaled duration; the two most-significant non-zero
  # units. Same presentation as lead-time, so the two tools' tables read alike.
  def duration_human(seconds)
    return "n/a" if seconds.nil?
    return "0s" if seconds <= 0

    s = seconds.to_i
    d = s / 86_400; s -= d * 86_400
    h = s / 3_600;  s -= h * 3_600
    m = s / 60;     s -= m * 60
    parts = []
    parts << "#{d}d" if d.positive?
    parts << "#{h}h" if h.positive?
    parts << "#{m}m" if m.positive?
    parts << "#{s}s" if s.positive?
    parts.first(2).join(" ")
  end

  # A pipeline/check-suite status string is "green" only when it CONCLUDED
  # SUCCESS. Everything else — running, failed, canceled, manual, and above all
  # ABSENT — is not green. `nil`/`""`/`"none"` deliberately land here: a change
  # request with no CI result at all has not been proven ready, and guessing in
  # the permissive direction is how a detector starts reporting work as
  # abandoned-and-finished when it is merely unbuilt.
  def green?(status)
    %w[success].include?(status.to_s.strip.downcase)
  end

  # THE predicate. Returns nil when the change request IS ready-and-idle, and
  # otherwise a symbol naming the FIRST reason it is not. A reason is returned
  # rather than a bare false so a caller can say what it looked at and why each
  # candidate was excluded — "how many candidates were considered versus
  # matched", per ~/dev/custom/CLAUDE.md -> "A failed lookup must never look
  # like an empty one".
  #
  # :unknown_activity is a MISSING measurement, not a disqualification on the
  # merits; the caller surfaces it as a probe failure rather than swallowing it.
  def disqualifier(mr, now:, idle_hours:)
    return :draft if mr[:draft]
    return :not_green unless green?(mr[:pipeline_status])
    return :blocking_discussions if mr[:blocking_unresolved]

    left = mr[:approvals_left]
    return :unknown_approvals if left.nil?
    return :approvals_pending if left.to_i.positive?

    secs = idle_seconds(mr[:last_activity], now)
    return :unknown_activity if secs.nil?
    return :recently_active if secs < (idle_hours.to_f * 3_600)

    nil
  end

  # Quality of a drift count. An uncountable range beats staleness: with no
  # number there is no bound to state.
  def drift_quality(count:, refs_refreshed:)
    return :unmeasured if count.nil?

    refs_refreshed ? :measured : :stale
  end

  # The ONE place a drift value becomes text. A bare integer here ALWAYS means
  # the refs were refreshed in THIS run — that invariant is the whole point,
  # and `>=0` vs `0` is the byte-identical-looking pair it exists to separate.
  def drift_display(count, quality)
    return "n/a" if count.nil?

    case quality
    when :measured then count.to_s
    when :stale    then ">=#{count}"
    else                "n/a"
    end
  end

  # True iff a drift value that was EMITTED is not exact. An empty row set is
  # never degraded: nothing was priced, so nothing can be misread.
  def degraded?(rows)
    rows.any? { |r| r[:drift_quality] != "measured" }
  end

  # Parse `origin/main@{2026-09-14T08:12:03-06:00}` out of the first line of
  # `git reflog show --date=iso-strict --format=%gd <ref>`. Returns nil for
  # `@{0}` (an ordinal, not a time) and for anything unparseable — a value that
  # is not a timestamp must never be PRINTED as one.
  def refs_last_moved(reflog_first_line)
    stamp = reflog_first_line.to_s[/@\{([^}]+)\}/, 1]
    return nil if stamp.nil?

    Time.iso8601(stamp)
    stamp
  rescue ArgumentError
    nil
  end

  # Build the emitted row for a qualifying change request.
  #
  # `drift:` is { commits: Integer|nil, quality: Symbol } — the count and how
  # far it can be trusted. Splitting the key is load-bearing: keeping a stale
  # number in `drift_commits` beside a sibling `drift_quality` would leave
  # `jq '.rows[].drift_commits'` — the obvious consumer — reading an
  # un-refreshed number as exact. `null` forces the question.
  def row(mr, now:, drift:)
    secs = idle_seconds(mr[:last_activity], now)
    quality = drift[:quality]
    count = drift[:commits]
    {
      id: mr[:id],
      branch: mr[:branch],
      target: mr[:target],
      title: mr[:title],
      head_sha: mr[:head_sha],
      pipeline_status: mr[:pipeline_status],
      last_activity: mr[:last_activity],
      idle_seconds: secs,
      idle_human: duration_human(secs),
      # EXACT count only. NEVER 0 for "could not count", and nil whenever the
      # refs backing it were not refreshed in THIS run. A consumer reading only
      # this key can never be handed an un-refreshed number.
      drift_commits: (quality == :measured ? count : nil),
      # Lower bound off un-refreshed refs: origin/<target> only GAINS commits
      # between fetches, so a stale count understates and never overstates.
      drift_at_least: (quality == :stale ? count : nil),
      drift_quality: quality.to_s,
      drift_display: drift_display(count, quality),
    }
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Impure shell — forge backends (not exercised by --self-test)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Every probe that fails is RECORDED, not just warned about. A probe that could
# not run and a repo with genuinely nothing to report both yield an empty row
# set, and an empty result on stdout with exit 0 makes them indistinguishable to
# the caller — the `ai/CLAUDE.md` class *A failed lookup must never look like an
# empty one*, and its corollary *a bar that cannot be MEASURED is a failure, not
# a pass with a note*. This tool exists BECAUSE nobody noticed three orphaned
# MRs for 2.5 days; a silent "0 orphans" from a broken probe would reproduce
# exactly that, with a citation on top.
#
# TWO LEDGERS, and the split is DENY-BY-DEFAULT. `record` is BLOCKING: it means
# the MEMBERSHIP of the row set is in doubt, so the run emits nothing and exits
# 3. `record_degraded` is for the one ENRICHMENT field — `drift` — whose
# failure leaves every orphan still listed, named and idle-timed, and costs
# only its price tag.
#
# The asymmetry is the point. If the FORGE list is incomplete, a missing orphan
# is invisible and silent — that is the 2.5-day outage this tool was written
# for. If DRIFT is unmeasurable, nothing is missing from the list. Suppressing
# the whole list because the price tag failed REPRODUCES the original outage in
# order to protect a column; measured 2026-09-21, when the shipwright's hourly
# cron lane (no ssh-agent, so `git fetch` dies on publickey) returned exit 3
# and zero rows while `glab` was answering fine and 25 change requests were
# ready-and-idle, the oldest for 54 days.
#
# `drift` is the ONLY enrichment field: every other key in `row` comes from the
# forge probe. That is what makes this a two-way split rather than a slope, so
# a probe added later without thought is blocking — the safe side.
module ProbeFailures
  class << self
    def list
      @list ||= []
    end

    def degraded_list
      @degraded_list ||= []
    end

    def reset!
      @list = []
      @degraded_list = []
    end

    def record(cmd, detail)
      list << { cmd: cmd, detail: detail.to_s.strip }
      nil
    end

    def record_degraded(cmd, detail)
      degraded_list << { cmd: cmd, detail: detail.to_s.strip }
      nil
    end

    def any?
      !list.empty?
    end

    def degraded_any?
      !degraded_list.empty?
    end
  end
end

def run_json(cmd, dir)
  printable = "#{cmd.first} #{cmd[1..].join(' ')}"
  out, err, st = Open3.capture3(*cmd, chdir: dir)
  unless st.success?
    warn "ready-and-idle: #{printable} failed: #{err.strip}"
    warn "Fix: run against a repo whose forge you can read (`gh auth status` / `glab auth status`)."
    return ProbeFailures.record(printable, err)
  end
  JSON.parse(out)
rescue JSON::ParserError => e
  warn "ready-and-idle: could not parse #{cmd.first} output: #{e.message}"
  ProbeFailures.record(printable, "unparseable output: #{e.message}")
end

def origin_url(dir)
  out, _err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "remote", "get-url", "origin")
  st.success? ? out.strip : ""
end

def detect_forge(dir)
  url = origin_url(dir)
  return :github if url.include?("github.com")
  return :gitlab if url.include?("gitlab.com") || url.include?("gitlab")

  nil
end

# ---- drift (git) ----------------------------------------------------------

# Refresh remote-tracking refs so `drift` can be measured at all. An open change
# request's head is on `origin` by definition, so after a successful fetch the
# sha is present locally; a fetch that FAILS is recorded, because every drift
# number in the run then rests on possibly-stale refs.
def refresh_refs(dir)
  _out, err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "fetch", "--quiet", "origin")
  return true if st.success?

  warn "ready-and-idle: git -C #{dir} fetch origin failed: #{err.strip}"
  # DEGRADED, not blocking: the orphan list is unaffected, only `drift`'s
  # precision is. The full explanation and the Fix: land in the DEGRADED SCAN
  # banner, once it is known whether any emitted row actually needed pricing.
  ProbeFailures.record_degraded("git -C #{dir} fetch origin", err)
  false
end

# Last time `origin/<target>` was observed to MOVE — a LOWER BOUND on how stale
# the refs are. git records no last-fetch time, so this deliberately does not
# claim one (*A claimed mechanism must be able to fire*). nil when unknown.
def refs_last_moved(dir, target)
  out, _err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "reflog", "show",
                                 "--date=iso-strict", "--format=%gd",
                                 "origin/#{target}")
  st.success? ? ReadyAndIdle.refs_last_moved(out.lines.first) : nil
end

# How many commits the target branch has gained since `sha` — the rebase debt
# accruing while the change request sits. nil (never 0) when it cannot be
# counted. Whether the returned number is EXACT or a lower bound is not this
# function's business: it depends on whether the refs were refreshed this run,
# which is one boolean for the whole run (see `drift_quality`).
def count_drift(dir, sha, target)
  if sha.to_s.empty?
    return ProbeFailures.record_degraded("git rev-list --count <sha>..origin/#{target}",
                                         "no head sha for the change request")
  end

  range = "#{sha}..origin/#{target}"
  out, err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "rev-list", "--count", range)
  unless st.success?
    warn "ready-and-idle: git -C #{dir} rev-list --count #{range} failed: #{err.strip}"
    # DEGRADED, not blocking: an unpriceable row is still a real orphan and is
    # still reported, as `drift=n/a`.
    return ProbeFailures.record_degraded("git -C #{dir} rev-list --count #{range}", err)
  end

  Integer(out.strip, exception: false)
end

# ---- GitHub (gh) ----------------------------------------------------------

class GitHubForge
  def initialize(dir)
    @dir = dir
  end

  def name
    "github (gh)"
  end

  def marker
    "#"
  end

  # One list call carries everything: statusCheckRollup is the rollup for the
  # PR's head commit, so it is by construction "green on the CURRENT head".
  def open_requests
    prs = run_json(%w[gh pr list --state open --limit 200 --json
                      number,title,headRefName,baseRefName,headRefOid,isDraft,updatedAt,reviewDecision,statusCheckRollup], @dir)
    return nil unless prs

    prs.map { |p| to_facts(p) }
  end

  def to_facts(p)
    decision = p["reviewDecision"].to_s
    {
      id: p["number"], title: p["title"], branch: p["headRefName"],
      target: p["baseRefName"], head_sha: p["headRefOid"],
      draft: !!p["isDraft"],
      pipeline_status: rollup_status(p["statusCheckRollup"]),
      # GitHub has no per-thread "blocking" flag reachable from `gh pr list`;
      # CHANGES_REQUESTED is the equivalent standing block, and it is the one
      # that actually stops a merge.
      blocking_unresolved: decision == "CHANGES_REQUESTED",
      # REVIEW_REQUIRED = a required approval is still unmet. APPROVED, or no
      # decision at all (no review rule on this repo), means nothing is waiting
      # on a human — the same reading as GitLab's approvals_required: 0.
      approvals_left: decision == "REVIEW_REQUIRED" ? 1 : 0,
      last_activity: p["updatedAt"],
    }
  end

  # "success" only when there is at least one check AND every check concluded
  # non-negatively. An EMPTY rollup is "none", never "success": a PR with no CI
  # result has not been proven ready (see ReadyAndIdle.green?).
  def rollup_status(rollup)
    checks = rollup || []
    return "none" if checks.empty?

    states = checks.map do |c|
      if c["__typename"] == "CheckRun"
        c["status"] == "COMPLETED" ? c["conclusion"].to_s.upcase : "PENDING"
      else
        c["state"].to_s.upcase
      end
    end
    return "pending" if states.any? { |s| %w[PENDING QUEUED IN_PROGRESS EXPECTED].include?(s) }
    return "success" if states.all? { |s| %w[SUCCESS NEUTRAL SKIPPED].include?(s) }

    "failed"
  end
end

# ---- GitLab (glab) --------------------------------------------------------

class GitLabForge
  PER_PAGE  = 100
  MAX_PAGES = 10

  def initialize(dir)
    @dir = dir
    @proj = project_path(dir)
  end

  def name
    "gitlab (glab)"
  end

  def marker
    "!"
  end

  def project_path(dir)
    # git@gitlab.com:group/sub/repo.git  OR  https://gitlab.com/group/repo.git
    require "uri"
    url = origin_url(dir)
    path = url.sub(%r{^git@[^:]+:}, "").sub(%r{^https?://[^/]+/}, "").sub(/\.git$/, "")
    path.split("/").map { |seg| URI.encode_www_form_component(seg) }.join("%2F")
  end

  def api(path)
    run_json(["glab", "api", path], @dir)
  end

  # The list endpoint already carries draft, updated_at, source/target branch,
  # sha and blocking_discussions_resolved. Only the survivors of those cheap
  # tests pay for the two per-MR calls (detail for head_pipeline, approvals) —
  # 40 open MRs would otherwise be 80 round trips to answer a question about
  # three of them.
  def open_requests
    listed = paged_open_mrs
    return nil unless listed

    listed.map do |m|
      base = {
        id: m["iid"], title: m["title"], branch: m["source_branch"],
        target: m["target_branch"], head_sha: m["sha"],
        draft: !!(m["draft"] || m["work_in_progress"]),
        blocking_unresolved: m["blocking_discussions_resolved"] == false,
        last_activity: m["updated_at"],
        pipeline_status: nil, approvals_left: nil,
      }
      next base if base[:draft] || base[:blocking_unresolved]

      base.merge(pipeline_status: head_pipeline_status(m["iid"]),
                 approvals_left: approvals_left(m["iid"]))
    end
  end

  # Paginate explicitly. A full page is indistinguishable from "that was all",
  # so stopping at one page would silently truncate the scan — the same class
  # of quiet wrongness this whole tool is aimed at.
  def paged_open_mrs
    all = []
    (1..MAX_PAGES).each do |page|
      batch = api("projects/#{@proj}/merge_requests?state=opened&order_by=updated_at" \
                  "&per_page=#{PER_PAGE}&page=#{page}")
      return nil unless batch

      all.concat(batch)
      return all if batch.size < PER_PAGE
    end
    ProbeFailures.record("glab api projects/#{@proj}/merge_requests (pagination)",
                         "more than #{MAX_PAGES * PER_PAGE} open merge requests; the scan was truncated")
    all
  end

  # `head_pipeline` IS the pipeline for the MR's current head — GitLab repoints
  # it on every push. Do NOT compare its sha to the MR's sha: with merged-result
  # pipelines the pipeline runs on a synthesised merge commit, so the two
  # legitimately differ and an equality test would exclude every MR forever.
  def head_pipeline_status(iid)
    d = api("projects/#{@proj}/merge_requests/#{iid}")
    return nil unless d

    d.dig("head_pipeline", "status") || "none"
  end

  def approvals_left(iid)
    a = api("projects/#{@proj}/merge_requests/#{iid}/approvals")
    return nil unless a

    # approvals_required: 0 satisfies this trivially, and correctly — the
    # question is whether anything is WAITING on a human, not whether a human
    # ever looked.
    (a["approvals_left"] || 0).to_i
  end
end

def forge_for(dir)
  case detect_forge(dir)
  when :github then GitHubForge.new(dir)
  when :gitlab then GitLabForge.new(dir)
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --self-test (pure logic only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def self_test
  failures = []
  check = lambda do |name, got, want|
    failures << "#{name}: got #{got.inspect}, want #{want.inspect}" unless got == want
  end

  now = Time.iso8601("2026-09-21T12:00:00Z")

  # duration_human — shared presentation with lead-time.
  check.call("dur 47m", ReadyAndIdle.duration_human(47 * 60), "47m")
  check.call("dur 2d10h", ReadyAndIdle.duration_human(210_960), "2d 10h")
  check.call("dur 0", ReadyAndIdle.duration_human(0), "0s")
  check.call("dur nil", ReadyAndIdle.duration_human(nil), "n/a")

  # idle_seconds — and the MISS side, which is the one that matters.
  check.call("idle 3h", ReadyAndIdle.idle_seconds("2026-09-21T09:00:00Z", now), 10_800)
  check.call("idle nil input", ReadyAndIdle.idle_seconds(nil, now), nil)
  check.call("idle empty input", ReadyAndIdle.idle_seconds("", now), nil)
  check.call("idle garbage input", ReadyAndIdle.idle_seconds("not-a-time", now), nil)

  # green? — everything that is not a concluded success is not green.
  check.call("green success", ReadyAndIdle.green?("success"), true)
  check.call("green SUCCESS", ReadyAndIdle.green?("SUCCESS"), true)
  check.call("green running", ReadyAndIdle.green?("running"), false)
  check.call("green failed", ReadyAndIdle.green?("failed"), false)
  check.call("green none", ReadyAndIdle.green?("none"), false)
  check.call("green nil", ReadyAndIdle.green?(nil), false)
  check.call("green empty", ReadyAndIdle.green?(""), false)

  ready = {
    id: 1187, title: "t", branch: "b", target: "main", head_sha: "abc123",
    draft: false, pipeline_status: "success", blocking_unresolved: false,
    approvals_left: 0, last_activity: "2026-09-19T01:24:00Z",
  }

  check.call("ready qualifies",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready, now: now, idle_hours: 2), nil)

  # Each disqualifier INDIVIDUALLY excludes, from an otherwise-qualifying row.
  check.call("draft excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(draft: true), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :draft)
  check.call("failed pipeline excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(pipeline_status: "failed"), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :not_green)
  check.call("running pipeline excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(pipeline_status: "running"), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :not_green)
  check.call("absent pipeline excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(pipeline_status: nil), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :not_green)
  check.call("unresolved discussion excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(blocking_unresolved: true), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :blocking_discussions)
  check.call("unmet approval excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(approvals_left: 1), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :approvals_pending)
  check.call("approvals_required 0 still qualifies",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(approvals_left: 0), now: now, idle_hours: 2), nil)
  check.call("recent activity excluded",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(last_activity: "2026-09-21T11:30:00Z"), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :recently_active)

  # An UNMEASURABLE input is its own outcome, never a quiet exclusion.
  check.call("unknown approvals is its own outcome",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(approvals_left: nil), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :unknown_approvals)
  check.call("unknown activity is its own outcome",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(last_activity: nil), now: now, idle_hours: 2), :unknown_activity)

  # --idle-hours boundary: exactly at the threshold counts as idle.
  at_2h = ready.merge(last_activity: "2026-09-21T10:00:00Z")
  check.call("exactly at threshold is idle",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(at_2h, now: now, idle_hours: 2), nil)
  check.call("one second under threshold is active",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(ready.merge(last_activity: "2026-09-21T10:00:01Z"),
                                       now: now, idle_hours: 2), :recently_active)
  check.call("same row excluded at a higher threshold",
             ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(at_2h, now: now, idle_hours: 3), :recently_active)

  # drift_display — the ONE renderer. A bare integer must mean "refreshed this
  # run" and nothing else. `0` vs `>=0` is the byte-identical-looking pair this
  # exists to separate; it is the exact case measured on 2026-09-21, and the
  # one that reads healthiest and is most tempting to "simplify" back.
  check.call("measured drift renders bare", ReadyAndIdle.drift_display(19, :measured), "19")
  check.call("measured zero renders bare", ReadyAndIdle.drift_display(0, :measured), "0")
  check.call("STALE zero renders as a lower bound, never as 0",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_display(0, :stale), ">=0")
  check.call("stale count renders as a lower bound",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_display(19, :stale), ">=19")
  check.call("unmeasured renders n/a", ReadyAndIdle.drift_display(nil, :unmeasured), "n/a")
  check.call("no number means no bound, even when stale",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_display(nil, :stale), "n/a")

  # drift_quality — one boolean for the whole run, plus "countable at all".
  check.call("counted with fresh refs is measured",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_quality(count: 3, refs_refreshed: true), :measured)
  check.call("counted with stale refs is stale",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_quality(count: 3, refs_refreshed: false), :stale)
  check.call("uncountable with fresh refs is unmeasured",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_quality(count: nil, refs_refreshed: true), :unmeasured)
  check.call("uncountable beats stale",
             ReadyAndIdle.drift_quality(count: nil, refs_refreshed: false), :unmeasured)

  # row — drift of 0 and drift of "could not count" must not render alike, AND
  # an un-refreshed number must never reach `drift_commits`, the key the
  # obvious consumer (`jq '.rows[].drift_commits'`) reads as exact.
  r0 = ReadyAndIdle.row(ready, now: now, drift: { commits: 0, quality: :measured })
  rn = ReadyAndIdle.row(ready, now: now, drift: { commits: nil, quality: :unmeasured })
  rs = ReadyAndIdle.row(ready, now: now, drift: { commits: 0, quality: :stale })
  check.call("drift 0 kept as 0", r0[:drift_commits], 0)
  check.call("measured drift has no lower-bound twin", r0[:drift_at_least], nil)
  check.call("unmeasured drift is nil, not 0", rn[:drift_commits], nil)
  check.call("unmeasured drift has no lower bound", rn[:drift_at_least], nil)
  check.call("STALE drift never reaches drift_commits", rs[:drift_commits], nil)
  check.call("stale drift is carried as a lower bound", rs[:drift_at_least], 0)
  check.call("stale drift says so", rs[:drift_quality], "stale")
  check.call("stale drift renders as a bound", rs[:drift_display], ">=0")

  # degraded? — an empty row set is NEVER degraded: nothing was priced, so
  # nothing can be misread, and a permanently non-zero exit trains the caller
  # to ignore the code.
  check.call("no rows is not degraded", ReadyAndIdle.degraded?([]), false)
  check.call("all-measured rows are not degraded",
             ReadyAndIdle.degraded?([{ drift_quality: "measured" }]), false)
  check.call("one stale row degrades the run",
             ReadyAndIdle.degraded?([{ drift_quality: "measured" }, { drift_quality: "stale" }]), true)
  check.call("one unmeasured row degrades the run",
             ReadyAndIdle.degraded?([{ drift_quality: "unmeasured" }]), true)

  # refs_last_moved — a value that is not a timestamp must never be PRINTED as
  # one (*A claimed mechanism must be able to fire*: git records no last-FETCH
  # time, only last-ref-MOVED, which is a lower bound on staleness).
  check.call("reflog timestamp is parsed",
             ReadyAndIdle.refs_last_moved("origin/main@{2026-09-14T08:12:03-06:00}"),
             "2026-09-14T08:12:03-06:00")
  check.call("an ordinal is not a timestamp", ReadyAndIdle.refs_last_moved("origin/main@{0}"), nil)
  check.call("empty reflog line is nil", ReadyAndIdle.refs_last_moved(""), nil)
  check.call("absent reflog line is nil", ReadyAndIdle.refs_last_moved(nil), nil)
  check.call("row idle human", r0[:idle_human], "2d 10h")

  # ProbeFailures — the "could not look" vs "looked, found nothing" accounting.
  # Asserted on the MISS side deliberately: a suite that only ever exercises a
  # healthy probe proves nothing about this class.
  ProbeFailures.reset!
  check.call("probes clean initially", ProbeFailures.any?, false)
  check.call("record returns nil (so callers still see no data)",
             ProbeFailures.record("gh pr list", "HTTP 401: Requires authentication"), nil)
  check.call("failed probe is observable", ProbeFailures.any?, true)
  check.call("failed probe names the command", ProbeFailures.list.first[:cmd], "gh pr list")
  check.call("failed probe keeps the reason",
             ProbeFailures.list.first[:detail], "HTTP 401: Requires authentication")
  ProbeFailures.reset!
  check.call("reset clears", ProbeFailures.any?, false)

  # TWO LEDGERS, deny-by-default. A degraded record must NOT make the blocking
  # gate fire — and this assertion is what fails if a FORGE probe is ever
  # rerouted through the degraded ledger, which would silently turn a
  # membership failure into "here is your complete list".
  ProbeFailures.record_degraded("git -C /repo fetch origin", "Permission denied (publickey)")
  check.call("a degraded probe does NOT block the scan", ProbeFailures.any?, false)
  check.call("a degraded probe is still observable", ProbeFailures.degraded_any?, true)
  check.call("degraded probe names the command",
             ProbeFailures.degraded_list.first[:cmd], "git -C /repo fetch origin")
  ProbeFailures.record("gh pr list", "HTTP 401")
  check.call("a blocking probe still blocks", ProbeFailures.any?, true)
  ProbeFailures.reset!
  check.call("reset clears BOTH ledgers",
             [ProbeFailures.any?, ProbeFailures.degraded_any?], [false, false])

  # GitHub rollup mapping (pure; no network).
  gh = GitHubForge.new(".")
  check.call("rollup empty is none", gh.rollup_status([]), "none")
  check.call("rollup nil is none", gh.rollup_status(nil), "none")
  check.call("rollup all success",
             gh.rollup_status([{ "__typename" => "CheckRun", "status" => "COMPLETED", "conclusion" => "SUCCESS" },
                               { "__typename" => "StatusContext", "state" => "SUCCESS" }]), "success")
  check.call("rollup in progress is pending",
             gh.rollup_status([{ "__typename" => "CheckRun", "status" => "IN_PROGRESS" }]), "pending")
  check.call("rollup one failure is failed",
             gh.rollup_status([{ "__typename" => "CheckRun", "status" => "COMPLETED", "conclusion" => "SUCCESS" },
                               { "__typename" => "CheckRun", "status" => "COMPLETED", "conclusion" => "FAILURE" }]), "failed")

  if failures.empty?
    puts "ready-and-idle --self-test: PASS (pure-logic assertions)"
    0
  else
    warn "ready-and-idle --self-test: FAIL"
    failures.each { |f| warn "  - #{f}" }
    warn "Fix: reconcile module ReadyAndIdle with the expected qualification/idle math above."
    1
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def usage
  warn <<~USAGE
    ready-and-idle — open change requests that are green, unblocked, approved, and unattended.

      ready-and-idle --repo DIR [--idle-hours N] [--json] [--no-fetch]
      ready-and-idle --self-test

    Fix: pass --self-test, or --repo pointing at a git checkout.
  USAGE
  1
end

def print_row(r, marker)
  # `drift_display` is the ONLY renderer of a drift value: a bare integer in
  # this column always means the refs were refreshed in THIS run.
  printf("%-6s  idle=%-9s  drift=%-7s  sha=%-9s  %-34s  %s\n",
         "#{marker}#{r[:id]}", r[:idle_human], r[:drift_display],
         r[:head_sha].to_s[0, 8], (r[:branch] || "")[0, 34], (r[:title] || "")[0, 46])
end

# The DEGRADED SCAN banner. Printed only when a drift value that was EMITTED is
# inexact, so it can never appear on a run that priced nothing.
def warn_degraded(repo:, rows:, refs_refreshed:, refs_reason:, target:)
  inexact = rows.count { |r| r[:drift_quality] != "measured" }
  warn "ready-and-idle: DEGRADED SCAN — the change-request list is COMPLETE. Every row"
  warn "  below is a real ready-and-idle change request, and membership was NOT"
  warn "  affected; #{inexact} of #{rows.size} row(s) could not be priced exactly, so only the"
  warn "  `drift` column is degraded."
  if refs_refreshed
    warn "  refs: refreshed this run"
  else
    warn "  refs: NOT refreshed this run (#{refs_reason})"
    moved = refs_last_moved(repo, target)
    warn "  origin/#{target} last observed to MOVE: #{moved || 'unknown (no reflog for the remote-tracking ref)'}"
    warn "    (that is the last time the ref moved, a LOWER BOUND on its staleness — git"
    warn "     does not record when a fetch last ran.)"
  end
  ProbeFailures.degraded_list.each { |f| warn "  - #{f[:cmd]}: #{f[:detail].lines.first.to_s.strip}" }
  warn "  drift column: `>=N` is a lower bound (the target branch only GAINS commits"
  warn "  between fetches, so a stale count understates and never overstates); `n/a`"
  warn "  could not be counted at all. A bare integer ALWAYS means the refs were"
  warn "  refreshed in this run."
  fix_head = if refs_refreshed || refs_reason.to_s.include?("--no-fetch")
               "drop --no-fetch so drift is measured against current refs"
             else
               "make `git -C #{repo} fetch origin` succeed so drift is exact — in a " \
                 "headless/cron session this is almost always a missing ssh-agent " \
                 "(`ssh-add -l` should list the key), so run the sweep from a session that " \
                 "has it or give the cron environment credential access to this remote"
             end
  warn "Fix: #{fix_head}. Until then ACT ON THE ROWS ANYWAY: they are complete and each"
  warn "  one is finished work nobody is carrying. Never quote a `>=N` drift as an exact"
  warn "  count. Do NOT report this run as unavailable — that is exit 3, and this is exit 4."
end

def main(argv)
  return self_test if argv.include?("--self-test")

  repo = nil
  idle_hours = 2.0
  as_json = argv.include?("--json")
  fetch = !argv.include?("--no-fetch")
  i = 0
  while i < argv.length
    case argv[i]
    when "--repo"        then repo = argv[i += 1]
    when "--idle-hours"  then idle_hours = Float(argv[i += 1], exception: false)
    when "--json", "--no-fetch" then nil
    else
      warn "ready-and-idle: unknown argument #{argv[i].inspect}"
      return usage
    end
    i += 1
  end

  return usage unless repo
  if idle_hours.nil? || idle_hours.negative?
    warn "ready-and-idle: --idle-hours must be a non-negative number"
    warn "Fix: pass e.g. --idle-hours 2 (the default) or --idle-hours 0.5."
    return 1
  end

  repo = File.expand_path(repo)
  unless File.exist?(File.join(repo, ".git"))
    warn "ready-and-idle: #{repo} is not a git worktree/checkout"
    warn "Fix: pass --repo pointing at a git checkout (e.g. ~/dev/custom, ~/dev/gen_saas, ~/dev/walt_ui)."
    return 1
  end

  forge = forge_for(repo)
  unless forge
    warn "ready-and-idle: could not detect a supported forge from #{repo}'s origin remote"
    warn "Fix: this tool supports github.com (gh) and gitlab.com (glab) origins. A repo " \
         "with neither (or with no `origin`) has no change requests to scan — there is " \
         "nothing here to report, and nothing was measured."
    return 1
  end

  refs_refreshed = fetch ? refresh_refs(repo) : false
  refs_reason = if refs_refreshed then nil
                elsif fetch then "git fetch origin failed"
                else "--no-fetch was passed"
                end

  now = Time.now.utc
  candidates = forge.open_requests || []
  rows = []
  excluded = Hash.new(0)
  candidates.each do |mr|
    why = ReadyAndIdle.disqualifier(mr, now: now, idle_hours: idle_hours)
    # An UNKNOWN input is a probe that did not land, not a verdict on the
    # change request. Record it, so the run refuses to call itself a scan.
    if %i[unknown_approvals unknown_activity].include?(why)
      ProbeFailures.record("#{forge.name} facts for #{forge.marker}#{mr[:id]}",
                           "missing #{why == :unknown_approvals ? 'approval state' : 'last-activity timestamp'}")
      next
    end
    if why
      excluded[why] += 1
      next
    end
    count = count_drift(repo, mr[:head_sha], mr[:target])
    rows << ReadyAndIdle.row(
      mr, now: now,
      drift: { commits: count,
               quality: ReadyAndIdle.drift_quality(count: count, refs_refreshed: refs_refreshed) }
    )
  end
  rows.sort_by! { |r| -(r[:idle_seconds] || 0) }

  # A scan that could not read the FORGE is NOT a scan. Refuse to emit a result
  # at all — an empty or partial row set here means "could not look", and the
  # caller must not be able to read it as "looked, found nothing". This tool
  # exists because a 2.5-day orphan went unnoticed; a quiet zero here would be
  # that failure again, self-inflicted.
  #
  # This gate covers MEMBERSHIP only, and runs FIRST. A failure that costs only
  # the `drift` column is handled below as a DEGRADED scan (exit 4), because
  # withholding a complete orphan list to protect a price tag is the very
  # outage this tool exists to prevent.
  if ProbeFailures.any?
    warn "ready-and-idle: SCAN INCOMPLETE — #{ProbeFailures.list.size} probe(s) failed " \
         "against #{repo}. This is NOT a measurement and MUST NOT be recorded as " \
         "\"0 ready-and-idle change requests\" or as a clean scan:"
    ProbeFailures.list.each { |f| warn "  - #{f[:cmd]}: #{f[:detail].lines.first.to_s.strip}" }
    warn "Fix: restore forge auth and local git refs for this repo, then re-run " \
         "(`gh auth status` / `glab auth status`; `git -C #{repo} fetch origin`; a cron or " \
         "other headless session often has no token even though an interactive one does). " \
         "Until it re-runs clean, report the scan as UNAVAILABLE, not as zero orphans."
    return 3
  end

  # Degradation is raised only if it affected an EMITTED value. With no rows,
  # no drift was computed, so nothing is misreadable — the run stays exit 0 and
  # says CLEAN SCAN. The un-refreshed state is still made observable, on the
  # CLEAN SCAN line itself; keeping the ordinary healthy sweep at 0 is what
  # stops a caller learning to ignore the exit code.
  degraded = ReadyAndIdle.degraded?(rows)
  banner_target = rows.first&.fetch(:target, nil) || "main"
  refs_note = refs_refreshed ? "refreshed this run" : "NOT refreshed this run (#{refs_reason})"

  if as_json
    puts JSON.pretty_generate(
      repo: repo, forge: forge.name, idle_hours: idle_hours,
      scanned: candidates.size, orphans: rows.size,
      excluded: excluded.transform_keys(&:to_s),
      refs_refreshed: refs_refreshed,
      refs_note: refs_note,
      refs_last_moved: (refs_refreshed ? {} : { banner_target => refs_last_moved(repo, banner_target) }),
      # The marker is in the PAYLOAD, so a consumer that ignores stderr still
      # cannot misread the state.
      degraded: (degraded ? ["drift"] : []),
      degradations: (degraded ? ProbeFailures.degraded_list : []),
      rows: rows
    )
    warn_degraded(repo: repo, rows: rows, refs_refreshed: refs_refreshed,
                  refs_reason: refs_reason, target: banner_target) if degraded
  else
    warn "repo=#{repo}  forge=#{forge.name}  idle>=#{idle_hours}h"
    if rows.empty?
      # Textually distinct from SCAN INCOMPLETE above, on purpose and by
      # construction: this line can only be printed after every MEMBERSHIP
      # probe landed, and only when no emitted value was priced.
      puts "ready-and-idle: CLEAN SCAN — looked at #{candidates.size} open change " \
           "request(s) in #{repo}; 0 are ready-and-idle." \
           "#{excluded.empty? ? '' : " (excluded: #{excluded.map { |k, v| "#{k}=#{v}" }.join(', ')})"}" \
           "#{refs_refreshed ? '' : " Note: refs were not refreshed this run (#{refs_reason}); " \
                                    "that did not affect this result, because no row needed drift priced."}"
    else
      warn_degraded(repo: repo, rows: rows, refs_refreshed: refs_refreshed,
                    refs_reason: refs_reason, target: banner_target) if degraded
      warn "ready-and-idle: #{rows.size} of #{candidates.size} open change request(s) are " \
           "ready-and-idle (finished work nobody is carrying)."
      rows.each { |r| print_row(r, forge.marker) }
    end
  end
  degraded ? 4 : 0
end

if $PROGRAM_NAME == __FILE__
  # Inside the entry guard, so requiring this file never exits on someone
  # else's ARGV. --help is answered BEFORE any other flag, on stdout, exiting 0
  # and doing nothing else (no network, no git, no write) — ai/bin/check-bin-help.
  if ARGV.include?("--help") || ARGV.include?("-h")
    puts <<~TXT
      Usage:
        ai/bin/ready-and-idle --repo PATH [OPTIONS]

      Report open change requests that are DONE and UNATTENDED: open, not a
      draft, green on their CURRENT head, with no unresolved blocking
      discussion, no unmet required approval, and no activity for --idle-hours.
      Nothing is waiting on CI and nothing is waiting on a human, so every
      second in this state is pure lead time.

      The sibling tool ai/bin/lead-time only sees MERGED change requests, so it
      cannot report this until the damage is already unrecoverable. This one
      fires while the work is still cheap to land.

      Columns:
        idle    time since the last activity on the change request
        drift   commits the target branch has gained since that green head sha
                (`git rev-list --count <sha>..origin/<target>`) — the rebase
                debt accruing while it sits. `n/a` means it could not be
                counted, which is never the same as 0. `>=N` means the refs
                were NOT refreshed this run, so N is a LOWER BOUND (the target
                branch only gains commits between fetches). A bare integer
                ALWAYS means the refs were refreshed this run.
        sha     the green head sha the pipeline/check suite passed on

      Notes:
        * A change request with NO CI result at all counts as not green, so it
          is never reported as finished-and-abandoned.
        * `approvals_required: 0` satisfies the approval test trivially, which
          is correct — the question is whether anything is WAITING on a human.
        * Read-only: it merges, approves, comments, relabels and resolves
          nothing. The only write is `git fetch` into local remote-tracking
          refs, which --no-fetch skips.

      Options:
        --repo PATH      git checkout to scan (e.g. ~/dev/walt_ui, ~/dev/custom)
        --idle-hours N   idle threshold in hours (default 2)
        --json           emit JSON instead of the human table
        --no-fetch       do not `git fetch` first; price drift off existing refs
        --self-test      pure-logic assertions (no network, no git)
        --help           this text

      Exit: 0 ok (including a clean scan that found nothing), 1 usage/tool
      error, 3 SCAN INCOMPLETE — MEMBERSHIP could not be established, no rows
      were emitted, and the run must not be read as zero orphans; 4 DEGRADED
      SCAN — the row list IS complete and actionable, only the `drift` column
      degraded to `>=N`/`n/a`. Exit 4 is not a failure: act on the rows.
    TXT
    exit 0
  end
  exit main(ARGV)
end
